    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded in 1849, Nopa was named after California state to celebrate the Gold Rush boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Long before settlers, the Ohlone people inhabited the Nopa area for thousands of years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Nopa"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Following the Mexican‑American War, Nopa shifted to American control and grew with Gold Rush settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Nopa"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "For most of its history, Nopa remained a working‑class neighborhood largely ignored by developers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Nopa"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nopa’s street grid blends orthogonal and diagonal streets, echoing its layered development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Nopa"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A historic Irish‑American presence shaped Nopa, evident in its bars and community groups.",
